aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{3})=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480658 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "537bf679-c812-55fa-b501-947966556962" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480658 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2,3.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -4 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -4 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480659 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480659 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97543" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480659 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480659 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ given by\n\n$$\nC=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{3})=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480659 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53cc1ac5-9894-5a1e-9795-bc748bbd204d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480659 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2,3.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}11 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480666 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480666 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38862" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480666 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480666 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $T:V\\to W$ is a linear map and $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ and $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ are the bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W$, respectively. Find the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T$ relative to these bases, if \n\n$$\n[T({b}_{1})]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],\\,[T({b}_{2})]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,[T({b}_{3})]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480666 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d02bc4b-8130-57f6-83ee-f4c23a8ee855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480666 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $C.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -9 \\\\ 8 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480680 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480680 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38863" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480680 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480680 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:V\\to W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x\\in V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480680 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a05da4cd-65c8-508f-a2ce-a7cea728523b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480680 #edb/ref 1074 "The coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480682 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480682 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38864" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480682 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480682 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $T:V\\to W$ is a linear map and $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ and $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ are the bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W$, respectively. Find the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T$ relative to these bases, if \n\n$\\,$ $T({b}_{1})=2{c}_{2}-3{c}_{1},\\,$ $T({b}_{2})=4{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,T({b}_{3})=7{c}_{2}-5{c}_{1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480682 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a7b00f3c-cf97-5a00-9aea-1a5f4575fc82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480682 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write the images of vectors in $B$ relative to the basis $C:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1})=2{c}_{2}-3{c}_{1} & \\,⟹\\,[T({b}_{1})]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2})=4{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2} & \\,⟹\\,[T({b}_{2})]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{3})=7{c}_{2}-5{c}_{1} & \\,⟹\\,[T({b}_{3})]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix  whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2})$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480694 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480694 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140426" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480694 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480694 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $T:V\\to W$ is a linear map and $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\}$ are the bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W$, respectively. Find the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T$ relative to these bases, if \n\n$\\,$ $T({b}_{1})=6{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}-7{c}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,T({b}_{2})=-5{c}_{2}+9{c}_{3}-4{c}_{1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480694 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d65cfc4f-129a-584c-86e4-865a0ea5e2d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480694 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write the images of vectors in $B$ relative to the basis $C:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1})=6{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}-7{c}_{3} & \\,⟹\\,[T({b}_{1})]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2})=-5{c}_{2}+9{c}_{3}-4{c}_{1} & \\,⟹\\,[T({b}_{2})]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and  $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ -1 & -5 \\\\ -7 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480702 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480702 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97577" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480702 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480702 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ -9 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:V\\to W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x\\in V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480702 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ee0c45a3-ebaf-5f7e-ac01-ecc7dd1f5cb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480702 #edb/ref 1074 "The coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ -9 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 27 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480711 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480711 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97589" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480711 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480711 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ (shown above) and a basis $C$ of ${R}^{3}.$\nThe matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to these bases, and the vector $x\\in {R}^{2},$ are also given. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480711 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc550b7f-6e92-5df1-a72d-54e40643e133" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480711 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $[x]_{B},$ the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B.$ So, let\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives us the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=1 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now row-reduce the corresponding augmented matrix using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480712 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186480576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480712 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97582" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480712 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480712 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ (shown above) and a basis $C$ of ${R}^{3}.$\nThe matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to these bases, and the vector $x\\in {R}^{2},$ are also given. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480712 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fcef5190-a033-5c84-9651-3cf919b057c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186480712 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $[x]_{B},$ the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B.$ So, let\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives us the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now row-reduce the corresponding augmented matrix using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 9 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
